<Project>

  <Import Project="..\..\Directory.Packages.props" />

  <!--
    Every version this project uses is the repository's; the one thing it does differently is not pin
    its transitive ones.

    Central transitive pinning holds Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions at the 10.0.0 floor
    Quartz ships, and the database drivers this project references pull a Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory
    that demands a higher patch, so restore refuses the graph with NU1109. A VersionOverride answers that
    for this project and breaks every measured run instead: BenchmarkDotNet generates a project per job
    beside the built exe, copies the package references into it and builds it — the in-process smoke run
    is the only run that skips this — and the generated project gets the centrally defined version while
    this one asks for the override, which is a downgrade either way round. The generated project sits
    under bin/, so a file here is what reaches both.

    Raising the floor instead is not on the table: it is a demand every consumer of every shipped package
    inherits, which is what the comment on those ids in the repository's own Directory.Packages.props says
    and what PackageDependencyTest holds. This project ships to nobody, so its transitive versions are its
    own business.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
